1
00:00:00,307 --> 00:00:03,523
Tonight, if a tooth starts to
throb, you'll reach for a pill.

2
00:00:03,543 --> 00:00:06,648
Tomorrow, a stranger in
a white coat will fix it.

3
00:00:06,668 --> 00:00:08,873
You'll lie back. You'll get an injection.

4
00:00:08,893 --> 00:00:10,750
And you won't feel a thing.

5
00:00:10,770 --> 00:00:15,390
If something worse happens, an ambulance
can be at your door in minutes.

6
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:17,031
You don't even think about it.

7
00:00:17,051 --> 00:00:19,331
There's a pharmacy around the corner.

8
00:00:19,351 --> 00:00:21,950
Thousands of medicines on its shelves.

9
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:23,833
Pills for pain. Creams for cuts.

10
00:00:23,853 --> 00:00:25,532
Drops for sore eyes.

11
00:00:25,552 --> 00:00:27,678
But medicine, as we know it, is new.

12
00:00:27,698 --> 00:00:32,867
Scientists only began to prove that germs
cause disease in the eighteen sixties.

13
00:00:32,887 --> 00:00:36,137
Penicillin was discovered
in nineteen twenty eight.

14
00:00:36,157 --> 00:00:40,075
It was first used to treat a
patient in nineteen forty one.

15
00:00:40,095 --> 00:00:44,380
Our species has been around for
roughly three hundred thousand years.

16
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,802
For almost all of that time,
there was no doctor at all.

17
00:00:47,822 --> 00:00:50,345
Now take time back fifty thousand years.

18
00:00:50,365 --> 00:00:53,322
A man wakes up in a cave,
with his jaw on fire.

19
00:00:53,342 --> 00:00:54,722
A tooth has gone bad.

20
00:00:54,742 --> 00:00:56,633
The pain spreads through his face.

21
00:00:56,653 --> 00:00:58,208
No pharmacy. No dentist.

22
00:00:58,228 --> 00:01:03,399
No painkiller. So he has to face something
you have never had to face alone.

23
00:01:03,419 --> 00:01:06,049
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

24
00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:06,949
They just suffered.

25
00:01:06,969 --> 00:01:09,957
Or they died. Life was short and brutal.

26
00:01:09,977 --> 00:01:12,622
Get hurt, get sick, and
you were left behind.

27
00:01:12,642 --> 00:01:14,298
At least, that's what we assume.

28
00:01:14,318 --> 00:01:16,697
But the evidence tells a different story.

29
00:01:16,717 --> 00:01:18,072
It's written in teeth.

30
00:01:18,092 --> 00:01:20,247
In broken bones that healed.

31
00:01:20,267 --> 00:01:23,824
And in skeletons of people who
should have died, but didn't.

32
00:01:23,844 --> 00:01:27,974
To understand how, we first have
to see what they were up against.

33
00:01:27,994 --> 00:01:30,126
Life without doctors was dangerous.

34
00:01:30,146 --> 00:01:35,121
Hunting meant getting close to big,
angry animals, with nothing but a spear.

35
00:01:35,141 --> 00:01:38,122
Neanderthal skeletons are
full of healed injuries.

36
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:39,762
Broken ribs. Broken arms.

37
00:01:39,782 --> 00:01:43,816
Damaged skulls. How do scientists
know these injuries healed?

38
00:01:43,836 --> 00:01:46,103
Living bone repairs itself.

39
00:01:46,123 --> 00:01:50,828
A break slowly knits back together,
and leaves a thick, smooth lump behind.

40
00:01:50,848 --> 00:01:54,914
If someone died right after an
injury, the edges stay sharp.

41
00:01:54,934 --> 00:02:00,366
If they're smooth and rounded, the person
lived on, for weeks, months or years.

42
00:02:00,386 --> 00:02:05,040
In the nineteen nineties, researchers
compared their injuries to those of rodeo

43
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:09,891
riders. A much larger study in twenty
eighteen found something simpler.

44
00:02:09,911 --> 00:02:15,241
Neanderthals had head injuries about as
often as early modern humans of the same

45
00:02:15,261 --> 00:02:17,815
time. Life was rough for everyone.

46
00:02:17,835 --> 00:02:19,601
And here's a myth worth breaking.

47
00:02:19,621 --> 00:02:22,880
You may have heard that ancient
people only lived to thirty.

48
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:24,780
That number is an average.

49
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:28,465
And it's dragged down by how
many babies and children died.

50
00:02:28,485 --> 00:02:31,556
Studies of hunter-gatherers
today find something else.

51
00:02:31,576 --> 00:02:35,559
If you made it through childhood, you
had a good chance of living into your

52
00:02:35,579 --> 00:02:37,440
sixties or seventies.

53
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:42,340
One large study of hunter-gatherer groups
found the most common age of death for

54
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,586
adults was around seventy.

55
00:02:44,606 --> 00:02:47,661
So the real question
isn't why they died young.

56
00:02:47,681 --> 00:02:50,730
It's how so many of them lived so long.

57
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:52,652
Because the dangers were everywhere.

58
00:02:52,672 --> 00:02:54,478
Some of them, you couldn't even see.

59
00:02:54,498 --> 00:02:56,691
A small cut could become infected.

60
00:02:56,711 --> 00:02:58,951
A bad tooth could poison the blood.

61
00:02:58,971 --> 00:03:02,352
They did have two defences they
didn't need to understand.

62
00:03:02,372 --> 00:03:03,725
The first was fire.

63
00:03:03,745 --> 00:03:08,562
Cooking kills many of the germs
and parasites hiding in raw meat.

64
00:03:08,582 --> 00:03:11,577
Every cooked meal was a
little bit of medicine.

65
00:03:11,597 --> 00:03:13,029
The second was fever.

66
00:03:13,049 --> 00:03:16,720
When the body fights an infection,
it turns up its own heat.

67
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:19,221
Many germs grow more
slowly when it's hotter.

68
00:03:19,241 --> 00:03:20,871
Even lizards do this.

69
00:03:20,891 --> 00:03:25,898
When they're infected, they move to warmer
spots to raise their body temperature.

70
00:03:25,918 --> 00:03:27,358
And then there was birth.

71
00:03:27,378 --> 00:03:29,784
Human birth is unusually difficult.

72
00:03:29,804 --> 00:03:34,649
A big baby head has to fit through a
narrow pelvis, twisting on the way out.

73
00:03:34,669 --> 00:03:39,199
And the baby usually comes out facing
away from its mother, which makes it hard

74
00:03:39,219 --> 00:03:41,323
for her to guide it out alone.

75
00:03:41,343 --> 00:03:46,110
So across almost every culture that's
been studied, women have had help giving

76
00:03:46,130 --> 00:03:51,333
birth. Some scientists think helping at
a birth may be one of the oldest forms of

77
00:03:51,353 --> 00:03:52,984
medicine there is.

78
00:03:53,004 --> 00:03:54,775
But here's something surprising.

79
00:03:54,795 --> 00:03:57,474
Humans weren't the only
ones to treat themselves.

80
00:03:57,494 --> 00:04:02,365
In nineteen eighty seven, in the Mahale
Mountains of Tanzania, researchers

81
00:04:02,385 --> 00:04:05,646
watched a sick chimpanzee named Chausiku.

82
00:04:05,666 --> 00:04:08,671
She chewed the bitter pith
of a shrub called Vernonia.

83
00:04:08,691 --> 00:04:10,199
It tastes terrible.

84
00:04:10,219 --> 00:04:12,045
Healthy chimps rarely touch it.

85
00:04:12,065 --> 00:04:14,584
Within about a day, she
was back on her feet.

86
00:04:14,604 --> 00:04:18,362
The local Tongwe people use
the same plant as medicine.

87
00:04:18,382 --> 00:04:22,812
Scientists later found that the plant
contains compounds that can work against

88
00:04:22,832 --> 00:04:25,826
parasites. Many animals also eat clay.

89
00:04:25,846 --> 00:04:29,242
Parrots in the Amazon gather
at riverbanks to lick it.

90
00:04:29,262 --> 00:04:34,294
Scientists think clay may soothe the
stomach, and soak up toxins from their food.

91
00:04:34,314 --> 00:04:37,180
People have done the same for a long time.

92
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,182
Pharmacies once sold a white clay
called kaolin, for upset stomachs.

93
00:04:42,202 --> 00:04:47,090
And in twenty twenty two, in a rainforest
in Sumatra, researchers watched an

94
00:04:47,110 --> 00:04:51,321
orangutan named Rakus, with
a fresh wound on his face.

95
00:04:51,341 --> 00:04:56,034
He chewed the leaves of a climbing
plant that local people use as medicine.

96
00:04:56,054 --> 00:04:59,565
He dabbed the juice onto his
wound, for about seven minutes.

97
00:04:59,585 --> 00:05:02,660
Then he covered it with the
chewed leaves, like a bandage.

98
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,861
The wound closed in five days.

99
00:05:04,881 --> 00:05:06,793
Within a month, it had healed.

100
00:05:06,813 --> 00:05:10,632
So if apes can find medicine,
what about our ancestors?

101
00:05:10,652 --> 00:05:12,447
Remember the man with the toothache?

102
00:05:12,467 --> 00:05:18,175
In a cave in northern Spain called El
Sidrón, archaeologists found the remains of

103
00:05:18,195 --> 00:05:19,914
a group of Neanderthals.

104
00:05:19,934 --> 00:05:22,199
About forty nine thousand years old.

105
00:05:22,219 --> 00:05:24,099
Their teeth were crusted with tartar.

106
00:05:24,119 --> 00:05:28,587
Tartar is hard. And it traps tiny bits
of whatever you put in your mouth.

107
00:05:28,607 --> 00:05:30,055
It's a time capsule.

108
00:05:30,075 --> 00:05:35,222
In one adult's tartar, researchers found
traces of wood smoke, cooked starches,

109
00:05:35,242 --> 00:05:37,491
and chemicals from two bitter plants.

110
00:05:37,511 --> 00:05:38,843
Yarrow. And chamomile.

111
00:05:38,863 --> 00:05:42,143
These plants have almost no value as food.

112
00:05:42,163 --> 00:05:46,139
But people have used them as
medicine for thousands of years.

113
00:05:46,159 --> 00:05:50,640
Other scientists say they could have
been flavouring, or eaten by accident.

114
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:55,765
Then, in twenty seventeen, a DNA
study made one Neanderthal stand out.

115
00:05:55,785 --> 00:05:58,664
He had an abscess you
can still see on his jaw.

116
00:05:58,684 --> 00:06:00,701
And a nasty gut parasite.

117
00:06:00,721 --> 00:06:03,097
In his tartar was DNA from poplar.

118
00:06:03,117 --> 00:06:08,222
Poplar bark contains salicylic acid,
a natural painkiller, related to

119
00:06:08,242 --> 00:06:12,340
aspirin. There was also
DNA from Penicillium.

120
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:15,576
The mould that penicillin
was later made from.

121
00:06:15,596 --> 00:06:18,444
The researchers think he may
have been treating himself.

122
00:06:18,464 --> 00:06:19,836
We can't know for sure.

123
00:06:19,856 --> 00:06:22,174
The mould may have come from old food.

124
00:06:22,194 --> 00:06:26,823
But a sick man, with a painkiller and
a mould in his mouth, is a remarkable

125
00:06:26,843 --> 00:06:30,360
coincidence. And people
kept using these plants.

126
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:34,710
Willow, a close cousin of poplar, carries
the same kind of painkiller in its

127
00:06:34,730 --> 00:06:38,273
bark. For thousands of years,
people chewed it, and brewed it.

128
00:06:38,293 --> 00:06:42,699
In eighteen ninety seven, chemists
turned that ingredient into a pill.

129
00:06:42,719 --> 00:06:44,215
We call it aspirin.

130
00:06:44,235 --> 00:06:48,811
Ancient Egyptian medical texts, written
more than three and a half thousand years

131
00:06:48,831 --> 00:06:53,288
ago, tell healers to dress wounds
with honey, grease and lint.

132
00:06:53,308 --> 00:06:57,248
Honey has so little water that
bacteria struggle to grow in it.

133
00:06:57,268 --> 00:06:59,766
The same trick that
keeps food from rotting.

134
00:06:59,786 --> 00:07:03,764
Now jump forward to about five
thousand three hundred years ago.

135
00:07:03,784 --> 00:07:06,578
High in the Alps, a man
died on a mountain pass.

136
00:07:06,598 --> 00:07:11,466
His body froze in the ice, and was
found by hikers in nineteen ninety one.

137
00:07:11,486 --> 00:07:16,592
We call him Ötzi. Ötzi carried two pieces
of birch fungus on leather strips.

138
00:07:16,612 --> 00:07:20,342
That fungus has chemicals that
may work against gut worms.

139
00:07:20,362 --> 00:07:22,474
And Ötzi had gut worms.

140
00:07:22,494 --> 00:07:24,721
He also had sixty one tattoos.

141
00:07:24,741 --> 00:07:29,596
Simple lines, mostly on his lower
back, knees, ankles and wrists.

142
00:07:29,616 --> 00:07:33,307
The same places where X-rays
show his joints were worn.

143
00:07:33,327 --> 00:07:38,318
His DNA even shows that he carried
the bacteria behind Lyme disease.

144
00:07:38,338 --> 00:07:41,942
Many researchers think the
tattoos were a kind of treatment.

145
00:07:41,962 --> 00:07:44,284
But no medicine could have saved him.

146
00:07:44,304 --> 00:07:47,323
An arrowhead was found
in his left shoulder.

147
00:07:47,343 --> 00:07:49,024
Here's the honest part, though.

148
00:07:49,044 --> 00:07:51,811
Most illnesses leave no mark on bones.

149
00:07:51,831 --> 00:07:55,535
A fever, a cough, a stomach
bug, they all disappear.

150
00:07:55,555 --> 00:07:58,909
So we only see a small
slice of ancient medicine.

151
00:07:58,929 --> 00:08:01,561
The parts that touched teeth and bone.

152
00:08:01,581 --> 00:08:05,487
If one thing hurt ancient people
again and again, it was teeth.

153
00:08:05,507 --> 00:08:10,435
At Krapina, in Croatia, about a hundred
and thirty thousand years ago, one

154
00:08:10,455 --> 00:08:12,757
Neanderthal had a twisted tooth.

155
00:08:12,777 --> 00:08:17,582
Next to it are grooves and scratches,
probably made by poking at the sore spot

156
00:08:17,602 --> 00:08:19,559
with a stick or a grass stem.

157
00:08:19,579 --> 00:08:24,984
About fourteen thousand years ago, at
Villabruna in Italy, a young man had a big

158
00:08:25,004 --> 00:08:26,745
cavity in a back tooth.

159
00:08:26,765 --> 00:08:30,295
Under a microscope, the
cavity shows scratch marks.

160
00:08:30,315 --> 00:08:33,446
Someone had scraped it out
with pointed flint tools.

161
00:08:33,466 --> 00:08:37,218
It's the oldest known evidence
of someone treating a cavity.

162
00:08:37,238 --> 00:08:41,725
About a thousand years later, at a
site in Tuscany, people went further.

163
00:08:41,745 --> 00:08:46,200
Someone widened the holes in a person's
two front teeth, and packed them with

164
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:50,846
bitumen, a sticky black tar,
mixed with plant fibres and hair.

165
00:08:50,866 --> 00:08:53,504
Probably the oldest fillings ever found.

166
00:08:53,524 --> 00:08:58,452
And about nine thousand years ago, at
Mehrgarh in Pakistan, people were drilling

167
00:08:58,472 --> 00:09:03,153
teeth. Archaeologists found eleven
drilled molars, from nine adults.

168
00:09:03,173 --> 00:09:07,689
In experiments, a bow drill with a flint
tip went through a tooth in less than a

169
00:09:07,709 --> 00:09:09,976
minute. And the patients were alive.

170
00:09:09,996 --> 00:09:13,617
The edges of the holes were worn
smooth by chewing afterwards.

171
00:09:13,637 --> 00:09:15,020
But here's the twist.

172
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,496
Tooth trouble got much worse
once people started farming.

173
00:09:18,516 --> 00:09:22,010
Soft, starchy grains feed
the bacteria that rot teeth.

174
00:09:22,030 --> 00:09:23,270
Teeth are one thing.

175
00:09:23,290 --> 00:09:25,153
But what about something bigger?

176
00:09:25,173 --> 00:09:30,290
In twenty twenty two, archaeologists
described a burial from a cave in Borneo

177
00:09:30,310 --> 00:09:31,732
called Liang Tebo.

178
00:09:31,752 --> 00:09:34,449
It's at least thirty
one thousand years old.

179
00:09:34,469 --> 00:09:36,834
The lower part of the
left leg was missing.

180
00:09:36,854 --> 00:09:38,098
It hadn't been bitten off.

181
00:09:38,118 --> 00:09:40,162
It had been cut. Cleanly.

182
00:09:40,182 --> 00:09:42,523
And the missing foot wasn't in the grave.

183
00:09:42,543 --> 00:09:44,821
It happened when this person was a child.

184
00:09:44,841 --> 00:09:47,759
And the bone had healed,
with no sign of infection.

185
00:09:47,779 --> 00:09:52,921
This child survived, and lived for another
six to nine years, in steep, rugged,

186
00:09:52,941 --> 00:09:54,680
rainforest country.

187
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:59,405
The researchers say whoever did it must
have known where to cut, how to control

188
00:09:59,425 --> 00:10:02,418
the bleeding, and how to
keep the wound clean.

189
00:10:02,438 --> 00:10:07,808
Before this find, the oldest known amputation
was about seven thousand years old.

190
00:10:07,828 --> 00:10:10,307
This one is more than four times older.

191
00:10:10,327 --> 00:10:14,807
The same region of Borneo has some of
the oldest known cave paintings in the

192
00:10:14,827 --> 00:10:17,651
world. How do you close
a wound without thread?

193
00:10:17,671 --> 00:10:22,728
In parts of Africa, South America and
Asia, people found a living answer.

194
00:10:22,748 --> 00:10:27,565
They held a big ant to the cut, let its
jaws bite down across the wound, then

195
00:10:27,585 --> 00:10:29,035
pinched off its body.

196
00:10:29,055 --> 00:10:31,534
The jaws stayed clamped
shut, like a stitch.

197
00:10:31,554 --> 00:10:34,559
And it's not the only
operation people survived.

198
00:10:34,579 --> 00:10:39,147
Across the world, archaeologists
have found ancient skulls with holes

199
00:10:39,167 --> 00:10:40,955
deliberately cut into them.

200
00:10:40,975 --> 00:10:46,193
One of the oldest healed cases, from
Ukraine, is over nine thousand years old.

201
00:10:46,213 --> 00:10:48,404
The bone around the hole had grown back.

202
00:10:48,424 --> 00:10:50,533
Which means the patient lived.

203
00:10:50,553 --> 00:10:56,243
In parts of ancient Peru, centuries later,
most patients survived the operation.

204
00:10:56,263 --> 00:10:57,850
So let's count the remedies.

205
00:10:57,870 --> 00:10:59,400
Bitter plants. Tree bark.

206
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:01,263
Honey. Toothpicks and flint.

207
00:11:01,283 --> 00:11:02,475
Fillings and drills.

208
00:11:02,495 --> 00:11:04,874
Ant jaws. And even surgery.

209
00:11:04,894 --> 00:11:08,744
But the most powerful medicine of
all didn't grow in the ground.

210
00:11:08,764 --> 00:11:13,745
At Dmanisi, in Georgia, archaeologists
found a skull nearly one point eight

211
00:11:13,765 --> 00:11:15,125
million years old.

212
00:11:15,145 --> 00:11:19,327
These are some of the earliest
humans ever found outside Africa.

213
00:11:19,347 --> 00:11:21,521
He had lost all but one of his teeth.

214
00:11:21,541 --> 00:11:23,209
Years before he died.

215
00:11:23,229 --> 00:11:25,512
The empty sockets had filled in with bone.

216
00:11:25,532 --> 00:11:28,127
Without teeth, chewing
tough raw food is hard.

217
00:11:28,147 --> 00:11:32,514
Some researchers think others may have
helped him, by softening his food.

218
00:11:32,534 --> 00:11:36,350
Others point out that some
toothless wild apes survive alone.

219
00:11:36,370 --> 00:11:40,551
In northern Spain there's a deep cave
shaft called the Pit of the Bones.

220
00:11:40,571 --> 00:11:45,376
Inside, archaeologists have found the
remains of at least twenty eight people,

221
00:11:45,396 --> 00:11:48,722
about four hundred and
thirty thousand years old.

222
00:11:48,742 --> 00:11:52,654
Among them, a child was born with
a skull that grew the wrong way.

223
00:11:52,674 --> 00:11:55,206
Researchers nicknamed the child Benjamina.

224
00:11:55,226 --> 00:11:59,602
Benjamina lived for more than five
years, maybe as many as twelve.

225
00:11:59,622 --> 00:12:03,902
The researchers think this child got
the same care as any other child in the

226
00:12:03,922 --> 00:12:09,503
group. And in Shanidar Cave, in Iraq,
there's a Neanderthal man known as Shanidar

227
00:12:09,523 --> 00:12:15,068
One. He was found in nineteen fifty seven,
by the archaeologist Ralph Solecki.

228
00:12:15,088 --> 00:12:18,006
He lived roughly fifty thousand years ago.

229
00:12:18,026 --> 00:12:21,412
At some point in his life, he
suffered terrible injuries.

230
00:12:21,432 --> 00:12:25,175
A crushed eye socket, that may
have left him blind in one eye.

231
00:12:25,195 --> 00:12:28,606
His right arm withered, and
his forearm and hand were lost.

232
00:12:28,626 --> 00:12:31,031
His leg injuries left him with a limp.

233
00:12:31,051 --> 00:12:33,197
And he was deaf in one ear.

234
00:12:33,217 --> 00:12:34,699
Yet his bones had healed.

235
00:12:34,719 --> 00:12:37,664
He lived for years after,
and died in his forties.

236
00:12:37,684 --> 00:12:42,189
The anthropologist who studied him said
his deafness alone would have made him

237
00:12:42,209 --> 00:12:44,807
easy prey. He couldn't have hunted much.

238
00:12:44,827 --> 00:12:46,729
He couldn't have run from danger.

239
00:12:46,749 --> 00:12:47,941
So someone fed him.

240
00:12:47,961 --> 00:12:49,054
Someone protected him.

241
00:12:49,074 --> 00:12:54,822
For years. And in northern Vietnam, about
four thousand years ago, a young man

242
00:12:54,842 --> 00:12:57,987
lived with a paralysis
that began in childhood.

243
00:12:58,007 --> 00:13:01,111
At least his lower body,
and maybe all four limbs.

244
00:13:01,131 --> 00:13:04,570
For at least ten years, he
depended completely on others.

245
00:13:04,590 --> 00:13:06,670
Someone had to feed him every day.

246
00:13:06,690 --> 00:13:09,723
Wash him. Turn him, so
he didn't get sores.

247
00:13:09,743 --> 00:13:14,098
The archaeologist Lorna Tilley calls
this the bioarchaeology of care.

248
00:13:14,118 --> 00:13:16,244
Reading kindness in old bones.

249
00:13:16,264 --> 00:13:19,012
And care may be why humans
live so long at all.

250
00:13:19,032 --> 00:13:23,736
Unlike other apes, human women often
live for decades after they stop having

251
00:13:23,756 --> 00:13:28,949
children. Among the Hadza, anthropologist
Kristen Hawkes watched grandmothers at

252
00:13:28,969 --> 00:13:33,638
work. They spent hours every day digging
food, and their grandchildren were

253
00:13:33,658 --> 00:13:35,015
better fed for it.

254
00:13:35,035 --> 00:13:40,038
Some scientists think grandmothers are
one reason our species lives so long.

255
00:13:40,058 --> 00:13:43,950
So were ancient people left behind
when they got sick or hurt?

256
00:13:43,970 --> 00:13:46,209
Many of them, clearly, were not.

257
00:13:46,229 --> 00:13:48,577
The oldest medicine wasn't a plant.

258
00:13:48,597 --> 00:13:50,956
It wasn't a sharp flint, or a drill.

259
00:13:50,976 --> 00:13:52,819
It was someone who stayed.

260
00:13:52,839 --> 00:13:54,480
Today, we have hospitals.

261
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:57,491
Antibiotics. Painkillers in every kitchen.

262
00:13:57,511 --> 00:14:00,233
We live longer than any humans before us.

263
00:14:00,253 --> 00:14:02,419
But doctors have noticed something old.

264
00:14:02,439 --> 00:14:07,005
People with strong friendships and family
ties tend to live longer than people

265
00:14:07,025 --> 00:14:12,028
who are isolated. One large review of
studies found the effect was comparable to

266
00:14:12,048 --> 00:14:14,759
well-known risks, like smoking.

267
00:14:14,779 --> 00:14:19,764
Think about that. We invented
antibiotics, scanners, and transplants.

268
00:14:19,784 --> 00:14:22,605
But the oldest treatment
we know of still works.

269
00:14:22,625 --> 00:14:25,501
A visit. A meal. Someone
who sits with you.

270
00:14:25,521 --> 00:14:30,051
Fifty thousand years ago, a man
sat in a cave with his jaw on fire.

271
00:14:30,071 --> 00:14:31,201
He had bitter plants.

272
00:14:31,221 --> 00:14:32,726
He had the bark of a tree.

273
00:14:32,746 --> 00:14:34,506
Maybe, a little mould.

274
00:14:34,526 --> 00:14:39,538
But what really kept people like him alive
was sitting next to them, by the fire.

275
00:14:39,558 --> 00:14:41,213
Your medicine comes in a box.

276
00:14:41,233 --> 00:14:43,308
Theirs sat beside you.
